# Philosophy Lab

**AI-written philosophy, and the work of deciding which arguments deserve further scrutiny.**

The project began with an AI philosophy competition and grew into a study of research methods: finding questions, developing arguments, using criticism, and preserving the reasons for each decision.

**100 original essays · 7 later essays · 23 historical reviewer profiles**

[Read the selected essays](reading/20261018/README.md) · [See all 22 decisions](docs/selection.md) · [Follow the history](docs/timeline.md) · [Open the logs](logs/README.md)

## Where the work stands

**2026-10-19.** The accepted reading package contains **10 essays from 22 candidates**. The next stage is the user's reading and deeper verification before choosing three essays and two reserves. The accepted record contains **110 individual reviews** (66 neutral and 44 profile reviews), plus **50 accepted reviews across 10 pair comparisons**. All 110 planned individual review slots were accepted.

Two initial attempts ended with capacity errors and no visible reviews. The work was paused and resumed on the same Astra configuration. A later local app-server initialization failure also produced no observed session or review; its repair was recorded separately. Two accepted replacements ultimately filled the original slots. The preserved failures and resumption are explained in the [execution account](docs/evaluation.md#capacity-interruption-and-resumption).

The [selection guide](docs/selection.md) explains the choices, exact versions, and remaining objections. The [reading packet](reading/20261018/README.md) contains complete texts without grades or selection labels.

## How the selection was organized

The accepted design gives neutral and judge-profile reviews a **60/40 balance**: three fresh neutral reviews and two fresh profile reviews per manuscript, using the same Astra model, ultra effort, and default service tier. A profile supplies instructions and source material; it is not a separately fine-tuned model or a validated forecast of the real jury.

